Define and declare Tiny Tcl's variadic functions with <stdarg.h> and ANSI
prototypes (docs/M1-REPORT.md, section 5.1).

Upstream defines Tcl_AppendResult, Tcl_SetErrorCode and Tcl_VarEval with
<varargs.h>, and tcl.h declares them as "f()" when compiling C, so callers
see no prototype. Open Watcom passes the arguments of an unprototyped call
in registers but reads those of a "..." definition from the stack, so
every error message corrupted memory (seen as writes into the ROM BIOS and
a corrupt MCB chain under DOSBox-X). Borland's default stack convention
hid this. panic(), a K&R function of nine fixed arguments called with
fewer, is made a vfprintf-based "..." function for the same reason, and
tclHash.c's "extern void panic();" is removed in favour of tclInt.h's
prototype.

diff -ru a/panic.c b/panic.c
--- a/panic.c
+++ b/panic.c
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
+#include <stdarg.h>
 
 /*
  *----------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ -36,17 +37,15 @@
  *----------------------------------------------------------------------
  */
 
-	/* VARARGS ARGSUSED */
 void
-panic(format, arg1, arg2, arg3, arg4, arg5, arg6, arg7, arg8)
-    char *format;		/* Format string, suitable for passing to
-				 * fprintf. */
-    char *arg1, *arg2, *arg3;	/* Additional arguments (variable in number)
-				 * to pass to fprintf. */
-    char *arg4, *arg5, *arg6, *arg7, *arg8;
+panic(char *format, ...)	/* Format string, suitable for passing to
+				 * fprintf, then its arguments. */
 {
-    (void) fprintf(stderr, format, arg1, arg2, arg3, arg4, arg5, arg6,
-	    arg7, arg8);
+    va_list argList;
+
+    va_start(argList, format);
+    (void) vfprintf(stderr, format, argList);
+    va_end(argList);
     (void) fflush(stderr);
     abort();
 }
